{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2504.20075, how does the HEAT toolkit address both stakeholder engagement and environmental sustainability in AI development?", "answer": "HEAT guides teams to engage diverse stakeholders throughout development and includes a section for assessing and mitigating environmental impacts, integrating both social and environmental concerns.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "Space 3 provides instructions on how to identify all relevant stakeholders, both direct (end users, decision subjects, system providers and deployers) and indirect (regulators, associated parties, civil society organisations), as well as to distinguish between deployers, users, and affected individuals, as per the Act's categorisation."}, {"page": 19, "quote": "For this reason, Space 2 includes a section dedicated to assessing and mitigating the environmental impact of AI systems. This section introduces users to methods for calculating the carbon footprint of a given model [29] as well as conducting a Life Cycle Analysis for AI systems."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2504.20075, what steps are recommended for AI teams to justify building a system before defining its intended use?", "answer": "Teams must first justify the need for AI by defining the problem, explaining why AI is best, and addressing criticisms, before documenting intended use.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "in Space 1, the onboarding section of the toolkit, we designed a series of tasks that guide the AI development team through a justification process of their AI system. These tasks include: ● Problem Definition: Define the problem, considering whether it is influenced by inequalities or injustices, and assess how AI might perpetuate or address these issues."}, {"page": 12, "quote": "It is only after this justification that defining and documenting the intended use of AI becomes meaningful."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2504.20075, what is the definition of 'consent' under Article 3 (59) of the EU AI Act, and how does the HEAT toolkit expand on it?", "answer": "Consent is defined as a subject's freely given, specific, unambiguous and voluntary expression of willingness; HEAT expands by advocating participatory agreement drafting and awareness of power imbalances.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "Article 3 (59) defines consent as \"a subject's freely given, specific, unambiguous and voluntary expression of his or her willingness to participate\"."}, {"page": 16, "quote": "agreements could instead be drafted with affected individuals through the methods outlined in stakeholder engagement and user centred design space."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2504.20075, what is the 'social model of disability' as introduced in Space 4?", "answer": "The social model of disability emphasises how design can create disability, shifting focus from individual impairment to societal barriers.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "introduce the social model of disability (emphasising how design can create disability),"}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2504.20075, what is the main critique of the term 'stakeholder' as used in the HEAT toolkit?", "answer": "The term 'stakeholder' is critiqued for its roots in extractive and violent colonial contexts, but is retained for clarity and lack of alternatives.", "evidence": [{"page": 14, "quote": "we are aware of the critiques of the term 'stakeholder', focusing on its roots in extractive and violent colonial contexts [42]. However, since no alternatives have been proposed that would serve as adequate umbrella terms denoting those who have an interest in or are affected - directly and indirectly - by a given project, for ease and clarity ... we have decided to refer to 'stakeholders.'"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2504.20075, what question does the toolkit ask to challenge the idea that data is neutral?", "answer": "The toolkit asks users to consider 'who designs it? Who benefits from it? and who is marginalised by it?' to challenge data neutrality.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "We ask users to confront how good data is context-specific by asking themselves 'who questions' about their AI systems: who designs it? Who benefits from it? and who is marginalised by it? [20]."}]}
]}
